otp — provenance note
=====================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`dtls`/`falcon`/`slhdsa` modules, which are likewise self-contained
crypto-adjacent modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

otp — clean-room implementation from RFC 4226 ("HOTP: An HMAC-Based
One-Time Password Algorithm", IETF, 2005) and RFC 6238 ("TOTP: Time-Based
One-Time Password Algorithm", IETF, 2011), covering: the HOTP
construction and dynamic truncation (RFC 4226 §5.3, worked example §5.4),
the digits/security considerations (§5.3, §7.3), and TOTP's time-step
mapping and resynchronization window (RFC 6238 §4.2, §5.2, §1.2 for the
SHA-256/SHA-512 extension). Both are public, open IETF specifications;
the algorithms are fully determined by the spec text (merger doctrine —
see CONVENTIONS.md §5). No reference implementation's source was ported
or consulted — the module was written from the RFC prose and the public
API of `std.crypto.auth.hmac` only (the RFCs' Java reference listings
were not used).

The test vectors (`src/kat_vectors.zig`) are transcribed from the RFCs'
own published conformance appendices — RFC 4226 Appendix D (secret,
counts 0..9, intermediate HMAC digests, truncated integers, 6-digit
codes) and RFC 6238 Appendix B (6 unix times × SHA-1/SHA-256/SHA-512,
8-digit codes). These are public specification artifacts, not copied from
any implementation's test suite. Note on the RFC 6238 secrets: the
Appendix B codes only reproduce with per-mode secret lengths (ASCII
"1234567890" repeated to 20 bytes for SHA-1, 32 for SHA-256, 64 for
SHA-512) — the errata-confirmed behavior of the RFC's reference program,
documented in `src/kat_vectors.zig`.

`std.crypto.auth.hmac.HmacSha1` / `.sha2.HmacSha256` / `.sha2.HmacSha512`
are used via their public APIs (MIT, part of Zig itself); nothing is
reimplemented.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: complete — HOTP, TOTP, formatting, and ±skew verification all
implemented and KAT-validated byte-exact against RFC 4226 Appendix D
(all 10 counts, including intermediates) and the full RFC 6238 Appendix B
table (all 18 rows)** (Debug + ReleaseFast).
